    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">What Is a Fajã, and Why Are There Farms at the Foot of Madeira&#x27;s Cliffs?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>14 November 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">A fajã is a patch of flat land at the bottom of a sea cliff, built from rock that fell from above or from lava that ran into the sea. The ground is fertile and the air at sea level is warm, so Madeirans have farmed these strips for centuries, often reaching them only by boat.</p>

      <h2>Where does the word fajã come from?</h2><p>Nobody is sure. It is an old Portuguese word of unclear origin, used across the Atlantic islands where Portuguese is spoken: the Azores, Madeira and Cape Verde. In Madeira it appears mostly in place names, and there are dozens of them. If a map label starts with "Fajã", you can be fairly sure there is a cliff above it and the sea in front.</p><h2>How does a fajã form?</h2><p>There are two ways, and the difference matters when you look at one from the water.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>How it forms</th><th>What it is made of</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Landslide fajã (detrital)</td><td>Part of a cliff collapses and the debris piles up at its foot, above the reach of the waves</td><td>Boulders, gravel and soil from the slope</td></tr><tr><td>Lava fajã (lava delta)</td><td>Lava flows over the edge of the coast and cools in the sea, pushing the shoreline out</td><td>Solid basalt, often with a low platform at sea level</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The famous fajãs on the south coast west of Funchal are of the first kind. A landslide fajã is born in a few steps:</p><ol><li>Rain, roots and time loosen a section of a high volcanic cliff, which on Madeira is made of layers of lava and ash cut by vertical dykes.</li><li>The section fails, sometimes in one night, and the rock slides into the sea at the foot of the cliff.</li><li>The waves sort the debris. They wash the finest material away and leave a beach of boulders and pebbles at the front.</li><li>Behind that beach, a flat or gently sloping platform stays dry. Smaller falls keep adding to it.</li><li>Soil builds up, plants arrive, and eventually people do.</li></ol><p>This is not ancient history. On the night of 1 to 2 February 1992, part of the cliff at Penha d'Águia, on the north coast between Faial and Porto da Cruz, collapsed and left a new strip of land nearly 200 metres long in the sea. Local papers reported it the next day as a new fajã.</p><h2>Why would anyone farm at the bottom of a cliff?</h2><p>Because on Madeira, flat ground is precious, and these are some of the warmest and best watered places on the island. A fajã sits at sea level, faces the sun and is sheltered by hundreds of metres of rock behind it. The soil is young, broken volcanic rock, which is rich. Farmers working the fajãs under Cabo Girão grow vines, mangoes, sweet potatoes, passion fruit and tomatoes. At Fajã dos Padres you see banana plants, papaya, avocado and guava.</p><p>The cost was access. For most of their history these fields were reached by sea, or by narrow paths cut into the cliff, and everything grown there had to be carried up on someone's back or loaded into a small boat.</p><h2>Which fajãs can you see from a boat west of Funchal?</h2><p>Two groups stand out, and both are on the coastal route from Funchal.</p><h3>The fajãs under Cabo Girão</h3><p>Below the 580 metre cliff, just west of Câmara de Lobos, a series of terraced fields runs along the shore. The regional geology survey describes the platform at the base, the Fajã dos Asnos, as built by landslides, one of them dated to 1930, and by blocks that still fall now and then. In front lies a beach of boulders and pebbles. In August 2003 a cable car was built from Rancho, on the clifftop, down to the fields, with two small cabins of six seats each. Farmers used it first. Visitors use it now too.</p><p>From the water this is the best way to grasp the scale. The farm huts look like matchboxes, and the cable looks like a thread hanging from the sky. It is also where the drone goes up on Chifbay trips when the weather allows, and from above the fields show as a thin green line between rock and sea. Our article on <a href="/posts/how-tall-is-cabo-girao">how tall Cabo Girão is</a> explains the cliff above them.</p><h3>Fajã dos Padres</h3><p>A little further west sits Fajã dos Padres, the fajã of the priests. Jesuit fathers farmed it from 1595 until the order was expelled from Portugal in 1759, and they made its Malvasia wine famous. A small Malvasia vineyard of about half a hectare still survives. For centuries the fajã was reached mainly by sea. A panoramic lift came in the late 1990s and a cable car from Quinta Grande followed, and today there is a small restaurant and a few houses to rent. See our <a href="/posts/faja-dos-padres-madeira-guide">guide to Fajã dos Padres</a> for visiting it on foot.</p><h2>Are there fajãs on the rest of the island?</h2><p>Yes, all around the coast. The most dramatic to visit by land is probably the Fajã da Quebrada Nova at Achadas da Cruz, in the north west, reached by a cable car that drops roughly 450 to 500 metres down an almost vertical slope. Many houses there now stand empty. And the north coast has the youngest one, the strip Penha d'Águia dropped into the sea in 1992.</p><h2>Is it safe to go close to a fajã by boat?</h2><p>Partly. The fields and the beach are as safe as the cliff above them, and a fajã exists precisely because cliffs here fall. Boats keep a sensible distance from the base of active cliffs like Cabo Girão and do not anchor right under them. Fajã dos Padres is different: it is a wider bay with people living and working on it, and it is where many south coast trips stop for a swim, with the boat well off the foot of the cliff.</p><p>On the Chifbay <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a>, the boat passes the fajãs under Cabo Girão for photos and drone shots, then stops at Fajã dos Padres so you can swim, jump in from the boat or take out a paddle board. The exact spot is the skipper's choice on the day, based on the swell. If that stop is the main reason you are coming, the <a href="/faja-dos-padres-boat-trip">Fajã dos Padres boat trip</a> page covers it in detail.</p><p>One honest note: a swim stop in front of a fajã is not the same as walking among its vines and eating lunch there. For that, take the cable car from Quinta Grande or from Rancho, and check the operating hours before you go.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>How do you pronounce fajã?</h3><p>Roughly fa-ZHAN, with a soft zh like the s in measure and a nasal ending.</p><h3>Can you drive to Fajã dos Padres?</h3><p>No. There is no road down. You reach it by the cable car from Quinta Grande, or by sea.</p><h3>Do people still live on the fajãs?</h3><p>Few people live on them all year now, but many are still farmed. Some, like Fajã dos Padres, also have rental houses and a restaurant.</p><h3>Is a fajã the same as a beach?</h3><p>No. A fajã is the flat land at the foot of a cliff. It usually has a pebble or boulder beach at its front, but the fajã itself is the ground behind it.</p>

      <blockquote>A fajã is what Madeira does with a fallen cliff: waits a few generations, then plants mangoes on it.</blockquote>
    </article>
